<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nayarit</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nayarit is a state on the Pacific coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with its capital at Tepic. It takes its name from Nayar, a Cora ruler whose remains were venerated in the mountains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-del-nayar">Sierra del Nayar</a>. Part of the colonial Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a>, the territory was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> as the Territory of Tepic in 1884 and became a state in 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nayarit.jpg" alt="Nayarit" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Tena · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aguamilpa_Taxi_(131575813).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nayarit is divided into 20 municipalities. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinaloa">Sinaloa</a> to the northwest, Durango to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> to the northeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> to the south, with a long Pacific coastline that includes the Marías and Marietas islands. The western ends of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-madre-occidental">Sierra Madre Occidental</a> break up its terrain, and it has two volcanoes, Ceboruco and Sangangüey. The Río Grande de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> is its largest river and is of major importance for irrigation. Coastal lowlands and river valleys were historically covered with tropical dry forest, and a network of lagoons and mangroves extends along the northern coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Radiocarbon dating suggests that the Aztatlán culture settled the western Mexican coast, including parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinaloa">Sinaloa</a>, Nayarit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, as early as 900 CE, with some evidence for as early as 520 CE. The Huichol and Cora, Uto-Aztecan peoples, lived in the region when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> became the first known European to enter it and claimed it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as part of Nueva Galicia. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a> the Spaniards took the region with considerable brutality, and the people revolted in what was later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a>. Spanish rule was also hindered by the terrain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-del-nayar">Sierra del Nayar</a>, and the last independent Cora communities were incorporated by force in 1722. Intense <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary efforts followed.</p><p>In the colonial period the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guna-yala">San Blas</a> was one of the most important trade ports on the American Pacific coast, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> arrived there before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> rose. The priest José María Mercado began the struggle for independence in Nayarit, taking Tepic and San Blas before royalists defeated and executed him. The first constitution of the Mexican Republic, in 1824, placed Nayarit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>. In the second half of the 19th century it was among the most turbulent territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with a population in open revolt and demanding land. It was one of the last territories admitted as a state of the federation, on 1 May 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nayarit is home to four Indigenous groups: the Wixaritari (Huichol), the Naayeri (Cora), the Odam (Tepehuan) and the Nahuatl-speaking Mexicaneros. The state is predominantly agricultural, producing beans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, tobacco, mangoes and other crops, and fishing is also important. The beaches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guna-yala">San Blas</a> and the Riviera Nayarit draw visitors, and the Islas Marías were designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nayarit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With independence in 1821 it passed to the First Mexican Empire, and its territory became the states of Jalisco, <b>Nayarit</b>, Aguascalientes and parts of others.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-new-galicia">Kingdom of New Galicia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cora were led by a ruler bearing the title Tonati, and their main temple on the Mesa del Nayar held the revered remains of an ancestral ruler, <b>Nayarit</b>, which gave the region its name.</blockquote><a href="/state/nayar">Nayar</a></li></ul></section>
